{
  "name": "Beach Sunset",
  "created": "2026-09-14T19:45:00.000Z",
  "notes": "A beach at sunset, bottom to top: sea, sand, sky. Each band is its own layer cut to its stretch of the strip by a soft `window` gate, so the bands blend where they meet -- the sea into wet sand, the sand into a glowing horizon.\n\nThe sea is a blue perlin. Its waves wash up the sand: a sine at about 0.09 Hz pushes the window's top edge up and down by a few LEDs, with `noise` roughening each wash and a perlin LFO on its rate so the waves arrive in sets rather than on a metronome. The same sine nudges the water's own drift, so the texture rides in with the wave. A thin white foam line follows the waterline, brightest at the top of each wash, and its `fade` warp sits above the Colour row with a 3.5 s release -- a retreating wave leaves a glistening streak on the sand that dims from white to pale blue.\n\nThe sand is a plain cream gradient, damp gold at the shore to near-white towards the horizon. It and the foam are the two palettes left at saturation and value 1, because a whitish colour is exactly what 2.0 would boost away into a pure hue.\n\nThe sky is two layers. Underneath, a steady `block` glow coloured by position, orange at the horizon deepening to crimson overhead, so the sky never goes out. Over it, a red-to-amber perlin, big-featured and boiling very slowly. A perlin's density is its noise, so on its own a dip would black out the whole sky; over the glow, the dips read as darker cloud instead. A slow perlin LFO drifts that field along the strip. A soft gold sun on `screen` sits just above the horizon. A sine of about 14 minutes sinks it towards the sand and dims it, and the same sine walks the sky's `indexBias` so the clouds turn crimson as it sets and amber again as it rises. A second perlin wanders that bias on its own, so no two dusks match.\n\nTide moves the whole shoreline -- sea edge, foam and sand -- up or down the strip. Colour turns the scene towards twilight: the sky towards rose and the sea towards violet.",
